     """Apply system_and_3 caching strategy to messages for Anthropic models.
 
     Places up to 4 cache_control breakpoints: system prompt + last 3 non-system
     messages, all at the same TTL.
 
+    Ted patch 17. ``system_only=True`` places the system breakpoint alone and
+    skips the three message-level ones. It exists for one-shot sessions — a
+    cron firing — where the message breakpoints cannot repay their write.
+
+    The three message breakpoints earn their cost across the turns of a
+    conversation: turn two reads back what turn one wrote. A cron session has
+    no turn two. It composes one reminder, delivers it, and is never resumed,
+    so every message-level cache entry it creates is written, billed at the 1h
+    rate of twice list input, and then expires having been read zero times.
+    Measured over 30 days: cron wrote 20.0M cache tokens and read 7.9M back —
+    0.40 reads per token written, against the ~1.1 a cached prefix needs to
+    beat not caching at all. 95% of cron's bill was cache writes.
+
+    The system breakpoint stays, and that is the point of doing this rather
+    than turning caching off for cron outright. Cron system prompts are
+    byte-identical between firings, so the bunched jobs — a dozen daily
+    reviews firing in one second at 21:00 — still read one another's prefix.
+    What goes is only the part that never had a reader.
+
+    ``cache_control`` is metadata. The model reads exactly the same text with
+    it and without it, so this cannot change a single word Ted says.
+
     Returns:
